WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.840
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Jesse Singer, who is a journalist and

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:10.260
also author of the book There Are No Accidents
The Deadly Rise of Injury and Disaster Who

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.940
Profits and Pay and Who Pays the Price.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.230
Jesse, great to have you on.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.230
I really appreciate it.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.860
Speaker 6: Thank you so much for having me,
David.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.440
Speaker 1: So, you know, in the context of
these, I'll start with something kind of micro

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.279
and then we'll go from there.

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:27.699
There's often these stories of, Oh, there
was an accident with a gun.

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:28.699
Someone was shot.

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:29.699
It was an accident.

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:34.710
A guy accidentally shot his daughter while
cleaning the gun or something like this.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.030
And we often will talk about on the show where.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:42.700
Okay, yes, we sort of understand socially
what the term accident means under the law.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.280
This is often called the negligent discharge
of a firearm.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.829
And sometimes it is something that people
are charged with and it's considered a crime,

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:52.719
at least in theory.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:58.390
But the term accident sometimes puts like
a very particular spin, as it may be on our

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.600
understanding of sort of like what happened.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.619
You talk about this at the corporate and the
industrial and other levels.

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:12.909
Talk a little bit about what does it mean
for something to be an accident in the context

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:18.810
of law and our understanding of what took
place to just kind of get us into the discussion.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:19.810
Speaker 6: Yeah.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.389
I mean, it's a really tricky term in that
sense because it means so many different things

00:01:25.389 --> 00:01:26.590
to so many different people.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:33.990
And then has this specific legal definition
of the line between intentional violence and

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:34.990
maybe an act of God.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.869
You know, someone who dies during a hurricane,
for example, totally out of our control.

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:47.149
And it is a way of, I found in the book absolving
certain people when things go wrong and punishing

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:48.209
others, you know.

00:01:48.209 --> 00:01:55.039
And so we see really different outcomes depending
on who's shot in the case of an accidental

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:58.000
discharge, you know, who the victim is and
who's gone.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.299
It was and whether or not we want to put blame
on them as a society.

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:04.619
And this even breaks down to the smallest
levels.

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:11.680
Like if you look at people being killed by
drivers and like jaywalking crashes.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:12.719
Yes.

00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:18.099
Or like drunk, drunk driving crashes in particular
where someone is, you know, struck by a driver.

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:24.269
The race of the victim correlates with sentence
length so that if the person struck by a drunk

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:30.519
crossing the street by a drunk driver is black,
the sentence length of the driver is significantly

00:02:30.519 --> 00:02:31.549
lower.

00:02:31.549 --> 00:02:37.099
So there isn't an area of accidental, so called
accidental death where, you know, we're not

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:44.200
seeing huge variations in how the term is
understood to to, you know, kind of equate

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:45.609
for different punishments.

00:02:45.609 --> 00:02:50.170
And then since so many of these things are
not individual acts of human error, but really

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:56.180
predictable, preventable, systemic causes,
we see the least culpability when it comes

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:03.439
to the actual corporate perpetrators of these
crimes, you know, and like how.

00:03:03.439 --> 00:03:07.540
The make and model of a gun and the design
of a gun and the way that the gun has not

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.049
been regulated is actually the real problem
here, rather than whether or not it went off

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:13.049
when you were cleaning it.

00:03:13.049 --> 00:03:16.129
Speaker 1: You know, there's this book I read
a few months ago.

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:18.260
I think it must be about 20 years old now.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:23.230
It's called normal accidents by a guy named
Charles Perot, I think is the pronunciation

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.650
of his last name.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.790
And in the book, he talks about a lot of different
areas.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.340
He talks about aircraft accidents.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.579
He talks about nuclear heat, all these different
areas.

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:39.370
And he kind of distinguishes what we might
call like systemic accidents, which are, in

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.010
a sense, predictable, where we might say,
here is a system.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.760
Here are the tolerances.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.420
Here are the ways the system can fail.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:52.819
And we can sort of make a prediction as to
how common these things will be.

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:54.430
And it's not going to be zero.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.049
Almost certainly it's not going to be zero.

00:03:56.049 --> 00:04:01.049
And one of the really interesting things he
writes about in the book is that as you add

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:06.930
mechanisms to try to make a system more safe,
the added complexity and the fact that you

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:14.410
now have systems interacting can actually
keep the odds of an accident, so-called roughly

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:17.489
the same, or sometimes even even kind of increase
them.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:23.940
We know that to some degree, these accidents
are predictable in systems because actuaries

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:28.810
in insurance, part of their job is to sort
of like put a monetary value and a number

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.250
on how likely are some of these things to
happen.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:38.349
On the on the issue of predictability and
preventable when it comes to industrial type

00:04:38.349 --> 00:04:39.699
stuff.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:47.030
We often will learn after the fact that industry
knew about a lot of these risks very specifically,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:51.160
and they either minimized it, covered it up,
whatever the case may be.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:56.680
What are the kind of counter pressures that
the public can sort of exert or the tools

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:01.550
that exist not after the fact to do a class
action lawsuit or a mass tort?

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:05.910
But in the lead up to these systems, sometimes
failing and what as you write about also are

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:06.910
predictable ways.

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:08.490
Speaker 6: Absolutely.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:09.490
Yeah.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.949
Perils like kind of one of the classics of
this field who really, like, gave us a framework

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:14.039
for understanding.

00:05:14.039 --> 00:05:20.410
And one of the things I try to do in the book
is take Pedro's basic ideas, this normal accident

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:25.520
theory, and by which he didn't mean normal
as in common, but but predictable.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.150
Yes, the inevitable consequence of our systems.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:35.229
And he meant that an accident is a thing that
exists in four walls, a nuclear power plant,

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:38.430
an oil tanker, this contained thing.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:44.270
But something I try to do is blow out that
theory to look at the much larger systems

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:46.570
we have that correlate directly to accidents.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:51.599
And that's income inequality, that's racism,
that's corporate power and a lack of union

00:05:51.599 --> 00:05:52.940
power.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.620
Because we talk about these things as random.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:59.419
And if it was true, then an injury related
death would be randomly distributed across

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:00.759
the country.

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:01.849
But it isn't true.

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:04.940
People of color, people living in poverty
are most at risk.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:11.139
And what we see is that most people who die
in accidents don't die in Charles Perot's

00:06:11.139 --> 00:06:16.539
accidents, a nuclear meltdown right now, ones
and twos every day on our roads, in our homes

00:06:16.539 --> 00:06:18.479
and in our workplaces.

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:24.000
And so to answer like what the solution is
at the core of it is that these accidents

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.970
where people of color and people living in
poverty are most at risk, are accidents where

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.100
policy and infrastructure make the difference
between life and death.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:35.300
So the safety of our roads, homes, the safety
of our roads, the safety of our workplaces,

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.509
and that's where we see these huge disparities
in accidental deaths.

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:44.419
So to name one, black people die in house
fires at twice the rate of white people.

00:06:44.419 --> 00:06:48.159
Indigenous people are struck by cars at twice
the rate of white people.

00:06:48.159 --> 00:06:52.560
So these aren't matters of individual error.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.820
These are matters of different conditions
at different people are facing whether or

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:01.280
not your apartment is a fire trap and whether
or not your government is regulating your

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.030
apartment and holding your landlord to account.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:09.560
And so the big picture answer is that, you
know, regulating corporate power and forcing

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:15.030
policy to protect everyone universally, primarily
through government regulation and access to

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:20.530
the social safety net, will reduce the way
that risk is currently unequally distributed

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:21.530
across the US.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:25.779
Speaker 1: It seems there's also sort of like
a psychological component, like if we think

00:07:25.779 --> 00:07:28.740
about drug overdoses as an example.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:33.430
Recently at his rallies, Donald Trump has
been to much praise from the audience talking

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:37.020
about we should do the death penalty for drug
dealers.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.509
They do it in China, a quick trial, 100% conviction
rate, you know, all sorts of authoritarian

00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:42.620
dystopian nightmares.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.919
But the crowd loves it and they cheer.

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:48.870
And there is something about when there is
an individual.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:55.949
Here's the guy on the corner who gave me the
drugs versus here's a pharmaceutical company

00:07:55.949 --> 00:08:01.009
that uses sales reps to encourage the overprescription,
which then you get from a doctor filled by

00:08:01.009 --> 00:08:02.460
a pharmacist.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:09.210
The personal blame seems much easier psychologically
when it's the guy on the corner to some degree,

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:13.800
and that's probably much to the delight of
the big pharma companies and their lobbyists.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:14.800
Right.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:15.800
Like that's bite.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:16.800
That's a feature for them.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.360
Speaker 6: Yeah, absolutely.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:23.439
And I think you often see, you know, today
from pharma companies, but throughout the

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:27.879
history of accidental death, whenever there's
a rise in one specific type of accidental

00:08:27.879 --> 00:08:32.120
death, you see corporations pushing these
bad people stories.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:33.120
Right.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.650
And so, you know, you see it directly.

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:42.030
I'm going to quote one of the Purdue pharma
leaders who was like, the problem here early

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:46.110
in the opioid epidemic is the criminal addicts,
the abusers.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.460
They're the problem, not how we're marketing.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:52.830
An addictive drug is not addictive, but these
individuals, and that is deeply comforting

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.210
for us as people.

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:59.200
We are comforted and feel permitted to move
on from what seems like random tragedy when

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.000
we can find a bad guy because it's a way for
us all to say.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.760
I wouldn't make those decisions.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.940
I'm not like that person that that person
who died, that person who hurt someone.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:12.780
I'm nothing like them because they're bad.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:18.720
And when we define them as bad, we can see
ourselves as good as a smarter as someone

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.340
who will do a little better under the same
circumstances.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:27.280
And this ranges from accidental death, where
we really do see a stigmatized bad person,

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:29.150
like a person who's addicted to drugs.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:30.150
Yeah.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:35.020
But also when, you know, on the little ones,
like a traffic crash or, you know, a fire

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:39.830
where a kid was playing with matches, finding
the bad person, you know, blaming the parent,

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:45.520
blaming the jaywalking pedestrian is a way
for us all to say, it can't happen to me.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:47.730
I make better decisions.

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:51.420
And we see throughout the history of accidental
death, even all the way back to the Industrial

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.290
Revolution, you know, when workers were dying
in untold numbers in factories and coal mines.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:10:01.590
We see industrialists pushing this idea of
the accident prone worker and this caricature

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:06.330
and stereotype of a drunk immigrant who doesn't
speak English.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:07.690
Sloppy worker.

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:13.040
That was really the cause of this massive
accidental death toll, when in fact it was

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:16.720
actually that the factories and the coal mines
were incredibly dangerous.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:21.481
And we know this because when we start to
regulate those factories and coal mines, accidental

00:10:21.481 --> 00:10:22.710
death on the job plummets.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:23.910
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:29.150
So speaking of that, I mean, I think it's
not at all a one, but it's an all of the above

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:31.170
for me, not a one of these things.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:37.620
We have ways in which regulation can make
things safer, whatever it is.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:43.941
Those things we're talking about are there's
also those who say to different degrees, technology

00:10:43.941 --> 00:10:49.520
is going to dramatically improve these things
as we learn more about how to design roads,

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:53.590
self-driving cars, you know, you have all
of these ideas that at some point technology

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.020
is going to make a lot of things much safer.

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:59.460
Technology in factories, that is going to
reduce injury, for example.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:05.670
The counter to that seems to be that the new
technology allows the limits to be pushed

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:07.360
for profit again.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:12.980
So like now you have sort of like the same
amount of safety, but there's more profit

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:14.150
and more productivity.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:16.610
And so you have to know kind of how to counterbalance
that.

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:21.010
But to what degree is it fair to believe that
technology will be a factor in making the

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:22.010
world safer?

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:25.330
Speaker 6: You know, so this is this is an
old story.

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:31.260
And there's always been huge advances in technology
that have reduced the accidental death toll.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:34.870
But only under one condition, which is when
they are regulated.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:35.870
Hmm.

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:39.790
And so if you look at technology like seatbelts,
airbags, collapsible steering column, these

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:41.240
were in the car.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:47.070
Huge advances in safety, but only once they
were regulated into every American automobile.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:51.580
And when they're not, when you have government
regulatory agencies that are afraid to regulate,

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:56.610
like Nissan is today, we get into a situation
where rich people survive and poor people

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:57.610
die.

00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:00.570
So like fully autonomous driverless cars are
a way off.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:05.360
But there's a wealth of life saving technology
that already exists, is tested and proven,

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:10.040
automatic emergency braking, intelligent assist,
speed governors that are regulated in other

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.400
countries where accidental death is far lower.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:14.850
But we don't mandate them here.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:17.350
Cars are under-regulated, so only the rich
are protected.

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:22.330
The rich pay for features that could say everyone
save everyone's life if the government forced

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:23.330
it.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:26.330
You know, and we see the same thing, you know,
as far back as the Industrial Revolution,

00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:31.460
you know, there were technologies, a few companies
were putting them forth, but the overall death

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:35.300
rates weren't falling because the government
wasn't forcing corporations to account.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.570
And when that happened, we saw results.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.480
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to talk about.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:48.890
Inevitably, one could imagine this kind of
caricature of the overregulated nanny state,

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:52.920
where we take everything you and I are talking
about to the absolute extreme.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:57.220
And, you know, science fiction movies are
made about it and it's all very, very scary.

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:59.720
And to some degree, I'm like sort of joking
about it.

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:04.390
But also I think even people who are in favor
of a stronger regulatory infrastructure would

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:08.640
concede that there's some limit at which we
say, Well, here are some choices we simply

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:14.430
allow people to make, but we want to limit
the collateral damage that might be associated

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:15.430
with those choices.

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:20.220
So it's really a choice that only affects
the person making the decision.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:27.360
How do we start to think about where on the
slider we sort of put that pin between the

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:33.800
idea of freedom and safety, or do you think
that that is not even a valid spectrum for

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:34.800
discussion?

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:42.080
Speaker 6: I think it is a notably new spectrum
to discussion, and I think we spend a little

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:43.310
too much time on it.

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:49.310
Just thinking about more than 200,000 people
every year in the US that are dying completely

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:53.350
preventable deaths and those numbers are infinitely
lower.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:59.140
In other wealthy countries, the rate of accidental
death is so much higher here because we have

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.650
this rhetorical conversation about freedom.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:05.270
But when it comes down to it, I think the
nanny state is wonderful if you don't want

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:06.300
to die an early death.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:07.820
I mean, what's a nanny?

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:10.690
It's someone that rich people pay to protect
their children from death.

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:13.980
We should all have that up.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:16.240
Americans will respond to what our leaders
promote.

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:22.160
This country did not start to reject the protections
of a benevolent government on its own.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.080
It's a product of corporate propaganda sold
through the mouthpiece of anti-government,

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:29.030
anti-regulatory presidents, and that's been
happening for decades.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:34.860
I don't think Americans want to die horrible,
painful early deaths in the name of freedom.

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:40.930
We just need politicians brave enough to protect
people and to tell a better story than this

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:43.830
anti-government, anti-regulatory propaganda
that we've been sold.

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:44.830
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:50.940
Crying about a nanny state often is a way
to kind of invalidate conversations about

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:54.470
just reasonable regulations that that should
exist.

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:57.160
It's sort of a way to kind of clamp down on
debates.

00:14:57.160 --> 00:14:59.050
It's kind of similar to.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:00.300
I'm offended, I'm afraid.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.190
Well, okay, the conversation's over then.

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:04.230
I guess it's a similar cudgel in a sense.

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:05.590
Speaker 6: Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:11.550
And it leaves us in this place where people
die incredibly preventable deaths every day

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:12.580
in ones and twos.

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:18.580
And we all tell a story about personal responsibility
and the fact that maybe they were bad people,

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:21.700
even though they are simply protectable.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:23.350
We're not going to fix human error.

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:25.660
We're not going to make people perfect.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:29.800
But there are so many ways we could protect
them from harm that we are disregarding for

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:34.850
a conversation about freedom in the nanny
state and the need for personal responsibility.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:41.600
Speaker 1: Just the last thing lowest hanging
fruit in terms of preventable death that could

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:44.510
be improved relatively simply or easily.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:45.810
We know what to do.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.940
What are the areas that people may or may
not know about?

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:51.540
Where the numbers are high and could be lowered
dramatically?

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:58.430
Speaker 6: It's true in every area of accidental
death by simply empowering our regulatory

00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:02.600
agencies so people are better protected and
reviving the social safety net so people can

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:07.610
afford to better protect themselves, you know,
not drive an old car, take the most dangerous

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.770
job, live in a fire trap apartment.

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:14.810
And because accidental death is so broad,
there are a million ways to do it in your

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:15.810
community.

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:19.530
You can advocate for traffic calming and public
transit expansions because you're far less

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:23.060
likely to die or to kill someone if you're
riding the bus than you are.

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:27.320
If you're in your own car, you can fight for
safe injection sites and the free distribution

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:33.830
of naloxone, which directly, dramatically
reduces accidental overdose deaths.

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:38.800
Pushing for ADA accessibility accessibility
like ramps and grab bars in your workplace

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:43.490
and in your apartment building is going to
reduce accidental death, making sure fire

00:16:43.490 --> 00:16:50.430
safety regulations are on the books and enforced
sprinklers, self-closing doors, all this stuff.

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:55.460
It doesn't reduce the likelihood of you making
a mistake or a kid playing with fire or someone

00:16:55.460 --> 00:17:00.370
not being a distracted driver just reduces
the likelihood that it's going to kill anyone.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:01.630
It just protects people.

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:06.520
Speaker 1: We have been talking with Jesse
Singer, who is a journalist and also author

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:11.809
of the book There Are No Accidents The Deadly
Rise of Injury and Disaster Who Profits and

00:17:11.809 --> 00:17:13.100
Who Pays the Price.

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:15.049
Jesse, really appreciate your time today.

00:17:15.049 --> 00:17:16.559
Speaker 6: Thanks so much for having me, David.

